---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Icons from "../../components/Icons.astro";
import Breadcrumb from "../../components/Breadcrumb.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="3 Days in Beijing: The Perfect Itinerary [2026]" description="Ultimate 3-day Beijing itinerary: Great Wall, Forbidden City, Temple of Heaven, and hidden hutongs. Day-by-day plan with subway routes and ticket tips.">
  <Breadcrumb items={[{ label: "Itineraries", href: "/collections" }, { label: "3-Day Beijing Itinerary" }]} />
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pt-6 pb-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-base-900 mb-3">The Perfect 3-Day Beijing Itinerary</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-base-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">Three days is the sweet spot for Beijing. You'll hit all the essential sights without feeling rushed. Here's exactly how to spend each day.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-8">
    <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-base-200 rounded-lg">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-base-100 text-base-500 uppercase text-xs tracking-wider">
          <tr><th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-left">Day</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-left">Morning</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-left">Afternoon</th><th class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-left">Evening</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-b border-base-200">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-base-900">Day 1</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/tiananmen-square" class="text-accent hover:underline">Tiananmen Square</a><br/>Flag raising at dawn</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/forbidden-city" class="text-accent hover:underline">Forbidden City</a><br/>3-4 hours exploration</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/jingshan-park" class="text-accent hover:underline">Jingshan Park</a><br/>Sunset panorama</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-base-200">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-base-900">Day 2</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/great-wall-mutianyu" class="text-accent hover:underline">Great Wall at Mutianyu</a><br/>Full morning hike</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600">Return to city<br/>Rest and refresh</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/nanluoguxiang" class="text-accent hover:underline">Nanluoguxiang</a><br/>Hutong stroll + dinner</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium text-base-900">Day 3</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/temple-of-heaven" class="text-accent hover:underline">Temple of Heaven</a><br/>Morning tai chi + park</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600"><a href="/attractions/lama-temple" class="text-accent hover:underline">Lama Temple</a><br/>Tibetan Buddhist art</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-base-600">Wangfujing Night Market<br/>Peking duck dinner</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Day 1: The Imperial Core</h2>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Start at <strong>Tiananmen Square</strong> at sunrise for the flag-raising ceremony. The square opens around 5am in summer. The flag-raising is brief but memorable - a military honor guard marches across the square to raise the flag exactly at sunrise. Arrive by 4:45am in summer to clear security and get a decent viewing spot.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">After photos, walk north through the Tiananmen Gate into the <strong>Forbidden City</strong>. Enter through the east gate (Donghuamen) instead of the main Meridian Gate to skip the worst of the queue. Allocate 3-4 hours. Don't try to see everything - focus on the central axis (Hall of Supreme Harmony, Hall of Central Harmony, Hall of Preserving Harmony) and pick one side gallery. The Treasure Gallery in the northeast corner is worth the extra ticket if you enjoy decorative arts.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Exit through the north gate and climb <strong>Jingshan Park</strong>'s hill directly across the street. The park entrance is CNY 2. It's a 10-minute climb to Wanchun Pavilion, and the panoramic view of the Forbidden City at sunset is the most photographed view in Beijing for good reason. Arrive 45 minutes before sunset for a railing spot.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Dinner:</strong> Walk 10 minutes east to Siji Minfu Roast Duck on Dengshikou Street. Expect a wait (30-45 minutes at peak), but it's worth it. Budget CNY 150-200 per person.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-500"><strong>Tip:</strong> Book Forbidden City tickets online at least 3 days in advance (7 days in peak season). You must enter your passport number. No on-site ticket sales.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Day 2: The Great Wall</h2>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Depart by 7:30am.</strong> Book a driver the night before (your hotel can arrange this, budget CNY 600-800 round trip) or take Bus 916 Express from Dongzhimen to Huairou, then a local taxi to <strong>Mutianyu</strong>. The journey is about 1.5 hours each way. Choose Mutianyu over Badaling for fewer crowds, gentler paths, and the toboggan slide.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Take the cable car up (CNY 100 round trip, enclosed gondola) and walk westward toward Tower 14. Spend 2-3 hours hiking between watchtowers. The views improve as you move away from the cable car station. By Tower 18, you'll likely have the Wall largely to yourself. Take the toboggan slide down from Tower 6 - it's a 1,580-meter metal track winding through the forest, and it's genuinely fun for all ages.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Return to the city by mid-afternoon. Rest at your hotel for an hour - you'll need it after hiking. In the early evening, head to <strong>Nanluoguxiang hutong</strong> for dinner and boutique shopping. Skip the main lane and explore the east-side alleys like Mao'er Hutong for quieter courtyard architecture and family-run dumpling shops.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Dinner:</strong> Guijie Street (Ghost Street) is a 10-minute walk from Nanluoguxiang. The entire street is lined with red lanterns and restaurants open past midnight. Try the crayfish at any busy spot, or Huajiayiyuan for Beijing cuisine in a courtyard setting.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-500"><strong>Tip:</strong> Pack snacks, water, and sunscreen. The Wall has limited food options and prices are high. Wear proper walking shoes - even the restored sections have uneven stones.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Day 3: Temples, Art, and Farewell</h2>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">Start at <strong>Temple of Heaven</strong> by 7:30am. Buy the through ticket (CNY 15) to access the Hall of Prayer for Good Harvests. But before entering the ticketed area, spend 30 minutes in the surrounding park - this is where you'll see hundreds of locals practicing tai chi, performing Peking opera arias, playing traditional instruments, and doing calligraphy with water brushes on the pavement. Sunday mornings are the most vibrant.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">After the Temple of Heaven (plan 2 hours total), take the subway 20 minutes north to <strong>Lama Temple</strong> (Yonghegong station, Exit C). This is Beijing's most active Tibetan Buddhist temple and a completely different atmosphere from the imperial grandeur you've seen so far. Incense fills the air, monks chant in the halls, and the 18-meter sandalwood Maitreya Buddha in the final hall - carved from a single tree brought from Nepal - is genuinely awe-inspiring. Allocate 1.5 hours.</p>
    <p class="text-base-600 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Farewell dinner:</strong> For your last meal in Beijing, two options. Splurge: Dadong Roast Duck on Tiantan Road (near Temple of Heaven, refined version of Peking duck, CNY 200-300 per person). Casual: Wangfujing Night Market area for a street food crawl, or any restaurant on Guijie Street for crayfish and cold beer.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-base-500"><strong>Tip:</strong> Pack your bag in the morning and check out before heading to Temple of Heaven. Most hotels will hold luggage. This saves you rushing back before a flight.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 pb-12 border-t border-base-200 pt-8">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Itinerary Variations</h2>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-base-900 mb-2">Short on Time? 2-Day Express</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-base-500 leading-relaxed">Skip Day 3. Do Tiananmen + Forbidden City on Day 1, the Great Wall on Day 2. You'll miss the temples but catch the two biggest highlights.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-base-900 mb-2">Slow Traveler? 3-Day Relaxed</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-base-500 leading-relaxed">Cut one attraction per day. Spend more time at each site and add leisurely meals. Swap Mutianyu for a shorter Wall visit.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
